
Keyhole v1.1.1 will be installed as follows.

  Container    200 (keyhole), unprivileged Debian 12
  Resources    2 cores, 1024 MB RAM, 8 GB on local-lvm, bridge vmbr0
  Reached by   tunnel
  Reached at   https://vault.example.com
  Binary       https://github.com/ssan9876/keyhole/releases/download/v1.1.1/keyhole-linux-${arch}
               verified against SHA256SUMS and its minisign signature
  Data         /var/lib/keyhole (SQLite; entirely ciphertext)
  Config       /etc/keyhole/config.yml
  Service      systemd unit "keyhole", running as keyhole
  Backups      nightly, by systemd timer "keyhole-backup", keeping the
               14 most recent snapshots in /var/lib/keyhole/backups
  Admin        me@example.com

Nothing has been changed yet.
RUN: pveam update
RUN: sh -c pveam available --section system | awk '{print $2}' | grep '^debian-12-standard' | sort -V | tail -n 1
  the newest debian-12-standard in that list is the template; it is downloaded if absent
RUN: pveam download local <newest debian-12-standard>
RUN: pct create 200 local:vztmpl/<newest debian-12-standard> --hostname keyhole --cores 2 --memory 1024 --swap 0 --rootfs local-lvm:8 --net0 name=eth0,bridge=vmbr0,ip=dhcp --unprivileged 1 --features nesting=0 --onboot 1
RUN: pct start 200
RUN: sh -c i=0; while [ $i -lt 60 ]; do pct exec 200 -- true >/dev/null 2>&1 && exit 0; i=$((i+1)); sleep 1; done; exit 1
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
apt-get update -qq
apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends ca-certificates curl minisign gnupg
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
rm -rf '/tmp/keyhole-install'
mkdir -p '/tmp/keyhole-install'
cd '/tmp/keyhole-install'
arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture)
curl -fsSL --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -o "keyhole-linux-$arch" "https://github.com/ssan9876/keyhole/releases/download/v1.1.1/keyhole-linux-$arch"
curl -fsSL --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -o SHA256SUMS "https://github.com/ssan9876/keyhole/releases/download/v1.1.1/SHA256SUMS"
curl -fsSL --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -o SHA256SUMS.minisig "https://github.com/ssan9876/keyhole/releases/download/v1.1.1/SHA256SUMS.minisig"
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
cd '/tmp/keyhole-install'
minisign -Vm SHA256SUMS -P 'RWQaHZz2jLPpRLK2aEj7/A/Rp0QtvRtJj/H8fjePukf3JMGPiyt91Ts6'
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
cd '/tmp/keyhole-install'
sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS --ignore-missing
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
cd '/tmp/keyhole-install'
arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture)
install -o root -g root -m 0755 "keyhole-linux-$arch" /usr/local/bin/keyhole
rm -rf '/tmp/keyhole-install'
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
umask 077
cat > '/usr/local/bin/update' <<'KEYHOLE_EOF'
#!/bin/sh
exec /usr/local/bin/keyhole update "$@"
KEYHOLE_EOF
chown 'root:root' '/usr/local/bin/update'
chmod '0755' '/usr/local/bin/update'
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
id -u 'keyhole' >/dev/null 2>&1 || useradd --system --home-dir '/var/lib/keyhole' --shell /usr/sbin/nologin 'keyhole'
RUN: pct exec 200 -- install -d -o keyhole -g keyhole -m 0700 /var/lib/keyhole
RUN: pct exec 200 -- install -d -o keyhole -g keyhole -m 0700 /var/lib/keyhole/backups
RUN: pct exec 200 -- install -d -o root -g root -m 0755 /etc/keyhole
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
install -d -m 0755 /usr/share/keyrings
curl -fsSL --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 https://pkg.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-main.gpg -o /usr/share/keyrings/cloudflare-main.gpg
chmod 0644 /usr/share/keyrings/cloudflare-main.gpg
echo 'deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/cloudflare-main.gpg] https://pkg.cloudflare.com/cloudflared bookworm main' > /etc/apt/sources.list.d/cloudflared.list
export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
apt-get update -qq
apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends cloudflared
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c "umask 077; cat > /etc/keyhole/tunnel-token"  < the tunnel token (not shown)
RUN: pct exec 200 -- chown root:root /etc/keyhole/tunnel-token
RUN: pct exec 200 -- chmod 0600 /etc/keyhole/tunnel-token
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
TUNNEL_TOKEN=$(cat '/etc/keyhole/tunnel-token')
export TUNNEL_TOKEN
cloudflared service install
systemctl enable --now cloudflared
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
umask 077
cat > '/etc/keyhole/config.yml' <<'KEYHOLE_EOF'
# Written by scripts/install.sh (Keyhole v1.1.1).
addr: 127.0.0.1:8477
data_dir: /var/lib/keyhole
base_url: https://vault.example.com
log_level: info
KEYHOLE_EOF
chown 'root:keyhole' '/etc/keyhole/config.yml'
chmod '0640' '/etc/keyhole/config.yml'
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
umask 077
cat > '/etc/systemd/system/keyhole.service' <<'KEYHOLE_EOF'
[Unit]
Description=Keyhole password manager
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User=keyhole
Group=keyhole
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/keyhole serve --config /etc/keyhole/config.yml
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=2

# The process needs one writable directory and nothing else. Everything below
# is what keeps a compromise of the HTTP handler from becoming a compromise of
# the container.
NoNewPrivileges=yes
PrivateTmp=yes
PrivateDevices=yes
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=yes
ProtectKernelTunables=yes
ProtectKernelModules=yes
ProtectControlGroups=yes
RestrictSUIDSGID=yes
RestrictNamespaces=yes
LockPersonality=yes
# Safe here, and worth keeping: the binary is CGO_ENABLED=0 static Go with no
# JIT and no plugins, so nothing it does needs a page that is both writable and
# executable. If something later appears to break under it, that is a reason to
# find out what is allocating W+X memory, not a reason to delete this line.
MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes
SystemCallFilter=@system-service
SystemCallArchitectures=native
ReadWritePaths=/var/lib/keyhole

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
KEYHOLE_EOF
chown 'root:root' '/etc/systemd/system/keyhole.service'
chmod '0644' '/etc/systemd/system/keyhole.service'
RUN: pct exec 200 -- systemctl daemon-reload
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
umask 077
cat > '/etc/systemd/system/keyhole-backup.service' <<'KEYHOLE_EOF'
[Unit]
Description=Keyhole nightly backup
After=keyhole.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
User=keyhole
Group=keyhole
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/keyhole backup --config /etc/keyhole/config.yml --keep 14

# The same hardening as keyhole.service. It is the same binary reading the same
# database and writing into the same one directory, so there is no reason for
# the backup job to run with more of the container available to it than the
# server does — and the job that reads every row is a poor place to relax it.
NoNewPrivileges=yes
PrivateTmp=yes
PrivateDevices=yes
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=yes
ProtectKernelTunables=yes
ProtectKernelModules=yes
ProtectControlGroups=yes
RestrictSUIDSGID=yes
RestrictNamespaces=yes
LockPersonality=yes
MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes
SystemCallFilter=@system-service
SystemCallArchitectures=native
ReadWritePaths=/var/lib/keyhole
KEYHOLE_EOF
chown 'root:root' '/etc/systemd/system/keyhole-backup.service'
chmod '0644' '/etc/systemd/system/keyhole-backup.service'
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
umask 077
cat > '/etc/systemd/system/keyhole-backup.timer' <<'KEYHOLE_EOF'
[Unit]
Description=Nightly Keyhole backup

[Timer]
OnCalendar=daily
# A container that was powered off overnight has missed its run. Without this
# the next backup is simply tomorrow's, and the gap is invisible until someone
# needs a snapshot from a day that has none.
Persistent=true
# Several containers on one host would otherwise all VACUUM INTO at exactly
# 00:00 and contend for the same disk.
RandomizedDelaySec=30m

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
KEYHOLE_EOF
chown 'root:root' '/etc/systemd/system/keyhole-backup.timer'
chmod '0644' '/etc/systemd/system/keyhole-backup.timer'
RUN: pct exec 200 -- systemctl daemon-reload
RUN: pct exec 200 -- /usr/local/bin/keyhole migrate --config /etc/keyhole/config.yml
RUN: pct exec 200 -- chown -R keyhole:keyhole /var/lib/keyhole
RUN: pct exec 200 -- systemctl enable --now keyhole
RUN: sh -c i=0; while [ $i -lt 30 ]; do pct exec 200 -- curl -fsS -o /dev/null 'http://127.0.0.1:8477/healthz' >/dev/null 2>&1 && exit 0; i=$((i+1)); sleep 1; done; exit 1
RUN: pct exec 200 -- runuser -u keyhole -- /usr/local/bin/keyhole admin create --email me@example.com --config /etc/keyhole/config.yml
RUN: pct exec 200 -- systemctl enable --now keyhole-backup.timer

That is the entire plan for Keyhole v1.1.1 in container 200. Nothing was changed.
The setup link is printed by "keyhole admin create" at the end of a real run.

The tunnel is registered. Point the Cloudflare public hostname
vault.example.com at http://localhost:8477 in the Zero Trust dashboard if
you have not already.

Worth knowing:

  Data          /var/lib/keyhole inside container 200 — item bodies, names and
                URLs are ciphertext the server has never held a key for
  Back up       nightly, shortly after 00:00 in the container's timezone, into
                /var/lib/keyhole/backups; the 14 most recent snapshots
                are kept and older ones pruned
                pct exec 200 -- systemctl list-timers keyhole-backup.timer
                pct exec 200 -- /usr/local/bin/keyhole backup   (one now, off schedule)
                (a snapshot is entirely ciphertext, so copying it somewhere
                less trusted than this host is a reasonable thing to do)
  Update        pct exec 200 -- /usr/local/bin/update
                (verifies the signature the same way this install did, and
                rolls back if the new binary will not answer /healthz)
  Logs          pct exec 200 -- journalctl -u keyhole -f
